window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"20-16","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"20-16-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-01/","title_he":"א – הנבואה","title_en":"Prophecy","body_en":"<p>Out of God's love for human beings, whom He created in His image, God chose the people of Israel to be His treasured people, and revealed Himself to the chosen of His people and granted them prophecy, in such a way that they can receive and understand His light and give it expression in speech, and through them all human beings can merit God's guidance and His blessing.</p>\n<p>Through prophecy the prophet merits rising beyond the limits of the body, connecting to God and cleaving to Him. Through this he merits attaining in his consciousness lofty apprehensions beyond the limits of the human intellect, and all at once he sees a complete and complex picture, through which he is elevated in understanding the divine matter revealed in the world and the ways of its conduct (Derekh Hashem, part 3, 3:4, 6). Through prophecy the prophet is filled with love and awe of God and strengthened in his connection to Him, and from then on, all his days, he will continue to long and yearn for the revelation of the divine light and for the visions of prophecy (Kuzari 4:3-5, 15; 5:22). And he will wish to reveal the image of God within him, to be a partner in the repair and perfection of the world, and to arouse his family, his friends, his people and all of humanity to cleave to faith and morality as the Torah guides.</p>\n<p>Prophecy is received in the imagination and clarified by the intellect, and the awareness received through it is certain and clear compared with the awareness received through the intellect. Therefore, through prophecy a person can understand the essence and meaning of life and faith more than through the intellect alone. However, since prophetic awareness is received with the help of the imagination, intellectual scrutiny must be joined to it, so that the person does not err in understanding the prophecy; therefore the prophet must be perfect in his intellect and acquainted with the sciences (Kuzari 1:11; 4:3, 11; Ha-ikarim 3:10; and see Middot Ha-Re'iya, Emuna 7).</p>\n<p>There are levels among the prophets. Some had prophecy for themselves, through which they rose in understanding faith and the meaning of the Torah, and thereby continued to rise in refining their character traits and in good deeds, and influenced those around them for the good. Most of the prophets, in addition to their personal stature, were sent by God to make His word known to other people or to the public. And some even merited prophecy intended for all generations, and these are the words of prophecy included in the books of the Prophets. The Sages said (Megilla 14a): \"Many prophets arose for Israel, double the number of those who left Egypt.\" However, the Men of the Great Assembly included in the Tanakh only the prophecies that were needed for the generations. The summit of prophecy was at the time of the Revelation at Mount Sinai, at which Moshe Rabbeinu and all Israel received the Torah, and by the power of this prophecy the world exists to this day and advances toward its redemption (below, halakha 10; above 1:7-8). It emerges, then, that prophecy is the foundation of the faith of Israel: through it the Torah was given, and through it God revealed Himself to Israel over many generations.</p>\n<p>A person cannot be a prophet unless he is wise and moral and refines his character traits to the utmost degree (below, halakha 7). Even so, it is not inevitable that he will be a prophet; only if the time is fitting for it and God chooses him will he actually merit receiving prophecy (below, halakha 1 [perhaps halakha 7 is meant]).<sup>1</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"<i>Navi</i> (prophet) derives from <i>niv</i> (speech), for the prophet speaks to the people in the name of God (Rashi, Shemot 7:1). Prophecy also has the sense of bringing, for the prophet brings the word of God to the people (Abarbanel, Yeshayahu 13:1). The prophet also speaks on behalf of human beings to God, like our forefather Avraham, who, after hearing the verdict of the people of Sedom, prayed for them; and like Moshe Rabbeinu, to whom Yitro suggested (Shemot 18:19): \"and you shall bring the matters to God\" (Ḥeshek Shlomo, Shorashim, \"ba\"). And likewise, \"And Moshe reported the words of the people to the Lord\" (Shemot 19:8). Prophecy can also be for the prophet himself (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:7), but it is named for speech, that is, the prophet's influence on his surroundings. This is like the Torah, whose central purpose is to extend its influence, and therefore it is called Torah after <i>hora'a</i> (instruction); and therefore the mitzva of Torah study was stated primarily as a mitzva to teach children and students, as it is stated (Devarim 6:7): \"And you shall teach them diligently to your children\" (Sifrei there). However, according to the Ramḥal, the essence of prophecy is the apprehension in the divine revelation, and the branch is that the prophets are sent to others (Derekh Hashem, part 3, 4:6; and see Da'at Tevunot 175-190; Kalaḥ Pitḥei Ḥokhma 5-11).<br>Because of the great importance of prophecy, two of the Rambam's thirteen principles of faith deal with it (6-7), because prophecy is the foundation of the Torah, and it is out of faith in prophecy that we received the Torah (above 1:21)."}]},{"slug":"20-16-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-02/","title_he":"ב – פועלו של הנביא","title_en":"The Work of the Prophet","body_en":"<p>Prophecy expresses the bond between God and Israel His people, and thereby it elevates Israel to cleave to faith and morality, to Torah and mitzvot, and sets forth a vision for the repair of the world (Sefer Ha-ikarim 3:8).</p>\n<p>As a continuation of this, prophecy also brings benefit to the conduct of the public and the individual. For example: our matriarch Rivka inquired of God about the sufferings of her pregnancy, and she was told that two nations were struggling and quarrelling in her womb (Bereshit 25:22-23). The wife of Yorovam son of Nevat went to Aḥiya the Shilonite to ask about her sick son (I Melakhim 14:1-4). Ben Hadad king of Aram inquired of Elisha the prophet whether he would recover from his illness (II Melakhim 8:7-10). The kings of Israel sometimes turned to the prophets with the question whether to go out to war, as when they sought to go to war against Aram in order to recover Ramot Gilad (I Melakhim 22:5-7). Likewise, when they went out to war against Moav and nearly died of thirst, they inquired of Elisha the prophet (II Melakhim 3:11). At times the prophets even led the people in its war, such as Devora the prophetess against Yavin king of Canaan (Shoftim 4:6-9), and Shmuel against the Philistines (I Shmuel 7:7-13).</p>\n<p>The prophets also prayed for the sick that they be healed, as God said to Avimelekh that if he returned Sara to Avraham, since Avraham is a prophet, he would pray to God for him and he would be healed (Bereshit 20:7). On the other hand, Aḥazya king of Israel sinned in that during his illness he inquired of the priests of the idol Ba'al Zevuv instead of inquiring of the prophets, and as a result he was punished and died (II Melakhim 1:2-4).</p>\n<p>A further role of the prophets was to rebuke sinners so that they would repent, such as the prophet who rebuked Eli the Kohen for the sins of his sons (I Shmuel 2:27); Natan the prophet, who rebuked King David for the sin of Bat Sheva (II Shmuel 12:1); and Gad the prophet, who rebuked him for counting the people (II Shmuel 24:11-13). Likewise, Eliyahu the prophet rebuked Aḥav: \"Have you murdered and also taken possession?!… Thus says the Lord: In the place where the dogs licked the blood of Navot shall the dogs lick your blood, even yours!\" (I Melakhim 21:19). And Yeshayahu, Yirmeyahu and Yeḥezkel rebuked the leaders and the people to repent, for otherwise the Temple would be destroyed, Israel would go into exile and the Land would be desolate.</p>\n<p>Since the prophets were close to God, at times God would perform miracles through them. With Moshe Rabbeinu the miracles were for all of Israel, such as the ten plagues, the splitting of the Red Sea, and the thunder and lightning at the Revelation at Mount Sinai; and with Yehoshua bin Nun, the splitting of the Jordan, the fall of the walls of Yeriḥo, and the sun standing still at Giv'on until the battle ended in victory. All this was in order to establish in the national and universal consciousness that God watches over the world and has chosen His people Israel, and there is none besides Him. As it is stated (Devarim 4:32-35): \"For ask now of the days past, which were before you, since the day that God created man upon the earth, and from one end of heaven to the other: has there been any such thing as this great thing, or has the like of it been heard? Did a people ever hear the voice of God speaking out of the midst of the fire, as you have heard, and live? Or has God ever attempted to go and take for Himself a nation from the midst of another nation, by trials, by signs and by wonders, by war, by a mighty hand and an outstretched arm, and by great terrors, according to all that the Lord your God did for you in Egypt before your eyes? You have been shown, so that you might know that the Lord is God; there is none else besides Him.\" And likewise it is stated after the splitting of the Jordan (Yehoshua 4:24): \"That all the peoples of the earth may know the hand of the Lord, that it is mighty, so that you may fear the Lord your God all the days.\"</p>\n<p>Afterwards, for hundreds of years, the books of the Prophets hardly tell of miracles performed by the prophets, for they were not needed (see below 18:7). However, as a result of the sins of the generation, it is told of the later prophets that they performed many miracles for private individuals, such as the healing of the waters by Elisha (II Melakhim 2:22), the bears that came out of the forest and struck the youths who mocked him (there 2:24), and the remembering of the barrenness of the Shunamite woman who hosted him and the reviving of her son (there 4:16, 34-35). Through these miracles they tried to arouse the people to repentance, in order to halt the decline that led to the destruction of the Kingdom of Israel (see Rav Samson Raphael Hirsch, Devarim 18:21-22).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-03/","title_he":"ג – הנבואה כתחליף לקוסמים ולמכשפים","title_en":"Prophecy as a Substitute for Diviners and Sorcerers","body_en":"<p>Prophecy had a further purpose: to prevent Israel from inquiring of the priests of the idolaters, who would foretell the future and promise success with the help of divination and sorcery. For man by his nature wishes to know the future and to seek help from higher powers, and if he does not find an answer within a proper framework, he will be drawn after the prophets of idolatry, diviners and sorcerers, who by various magical powers foretell the future and give guidance. Therefore God gave the prophets the ability to help people, and by answering their questions they would be able to elevate their faith and guide them in the service of God. As it is stated (Devarim 18:9-15): \"When you come into the land which the Lord your God gives you, you shall not learn to do according to the abominations of those nations. There shall not be found among you anyone who makes his son or his daughter pass through the fire, one who practises divination, a soothsayer, an enchanter or a sorcerer, or a charmer, or one who consults a ghost or a familiar spirit, or one who inquires of the dead. For whoever does these things is an abomination to the Lord, and because of these abominations the Lord your God is driving them out from before you. You shall be wholehearted with the Lord your God. For these nations, which you are about to dispossess, listen to soothsayers and to diviners; but as for you, the Lord your God has not permitted you so. A prophet from your midst, of your brethren, like me, will the Lord your God raise up for you; to him you shall listen\" (Ramban and Sforno there; Rambam in the Introduction to the Mishna; below 19:12, n. 12).</p>\n<p>Thus we find that people would ask prophets about earthly matters, such as Shaul, who went to ask Shmuel where the donkeys that his father had lost were (I Shmuel 9:6). The prominent advantage of God's prophets over the sorcerers and diviners is that they are true prophets, that is, absolutely faithful to the truth, and when they received a prophecy, all its details were completely precise. Whereas the forecast of the sorcerers and diviners is not precise in all its details, and when they were in doubt, they made their answer vague, so that if something other than what they foretold should happen, they could claim that this was what they had meant (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:3). It is true that sometimes, for various reasons, God did not reveal the answer to the prophet, and since the prophets refrained from answering about something they had not received in prophecy, the questioner faced the temptation to go to false prophets, sorcerers and diviners, who always answered. Even King Shaul, who fought against those who consult ghosts and familiar spirits and eradicated them from the land, when, as a result of his sin, God did not answer him through the prophets, out of his great desire to know whether to go out to war against the Philistines, sinned and went secretly to consult a woman who conjured ghosts (I Shmuel 28; below 26:4-5). It is worth noting that even when the prophets did not receive a prophetic answer, they could give those who asked them good advice as sages, while emphasising that this was advice and not prophecy.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-04/","title_he":"ד – הנבואה בישראל","title_en":"Prophecy in Israel","body_en":"<p>The most characteristic trait of the people of Israel is the desire to add goodness and blessing to the world, by revealing the word of God and His blessing through settling the Land of Israel and doing righteousness and justice (see above 1:2-3). Therefore this was the content of many of the divine revelations to the Patriarchs, first among them our forefather Avraham. As it is stated (Bereshit 12:1-3): \"And the Lord said to Avram: Go forth from your land, and from your birthplace, and from your father's house, to the land that I will show you. And I will make of you a great nation, and I will bless you and make your name great, and you shall be a blessing. And I will bless those who bless you, and him who curses you I will curse; and in you shall all the families of the earth be blessed.\" And it is stated (there 18:18-19): \"And Avraham shall surely become a great and mighty nation, and all the nations of the earth shall be blessed in him. For I have known him, so that he will command his children and his household after him, and they will keep the way of the Lord, to do righteousness and justice.\"</p>\n<p>Since prophecy reveals the word of God and His guidance for the repair of the world and its redemption, the talent for prophecy is the talent most characteristic of the people of Israel. As Rabbi Yehuda Halevi explained in his book, the Kuzari, the trait of prophecy is the special virtue and <i>segula</i> (treasured quality) of Israel, and about this it is stated (Shemot 19:5): \"And you shall be My treasure from among all the peoples.\" Therefore at the Revelation at Mount Sinai all Israel – men and women, old and young – merited a level of prophecy that has no equal. And although as a rule only one who is wise and of good character can merit prophecy, God wished at the Revelation at Mount Sinai to reveal to Israel their special quality: that all of them are fit to rise in wisdom and in the refinement of their character and to merit prophecy (Kuzari 1:41-43, 103, 109). This is what Moshe Rabbeinu said (Bamidbar 11:29): \"Would that all the Lord's people were prophets, that the Lord would put His spirit upon them.\" And so it will be in the future, that all Israel will merit prophecy, as it is stated (Yoel 3:1): \"And it shall come to pass afterward that I will pour out My spirit upon all flesh, and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy; your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions.\" And it is stated (Yirmeyahu 31:33): \"And they shall no longer teach every man his neighbour and every man his brother, saying: Know the Lord; for they shall all know Me, from the least of them to the greatest of them, says the Lord.\" And thus all will merit an abundance of light and life, and will add goodness and blessing to the world, each according to his special character.</p>\n<p>All the prophecies are by the merit of Israel, because prophecy is the revelation of God's word in order to benefit the world, and for this purpose God chose His people Israel, to bring His word and His blessing through them. And when Israel is unable to act openly for the betterment and repair of the world, prophecy is not revealed. Even with Moshe Rabbeinu God spoke only by the merit of Israel, and therefore after they sinned with the sin of the Golden Calf, God said to Moshe: \"Go, get down, for your people have acted corruptly\" (Shemot 32:7), for \"I gave you greatness only for the sake of Israel! And now that Israel has sinned – what do I need you for?!\" (Berakhot 32a). They further said: \"And not only with Moshe did God speak by the merit of Israel, but with all the prophets God spoke only by the merit of Israel\" (Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael, Parashat Bo). And the Sages likewise said that Hillel the Elder was fit to receive prophecy, but his generation was not worthy of it, and therefore he did not receive prophecy (Sanhedrin 11a).</p>\n<p>The level of prophecy too depends on the state of Israel. When Israel is worthy of a revelation of the Shekhina at a high level, those who are fit for it merit prophecy at a high level, and when Israel is at a less exalted level, their prophets too receive prophecy at a less exalted level. Therefore the early prophets saw through \"a luminous speculum within a non-luminous one\" (below, halakha 9), whereas the later ones, such as Yeshayahu and Yirmeyahu, who already lived in the period leading up to the destruction of the Temple, saw \"through a non-luminous speculum within a non-luminous one\" (Gra, Yeshayahu 1:1). And the prophets who prophesied in the days of the exile and at the beginning of the Second Temple period prophesied at an even lower level. Therefore the prophecies of Zekharya in the days of the Second Temple came through \"the angel who spoke with me\" (Ḥagiga 13b, Rashi; Ramban in the sermon \"Torat Hashem Temima\"). And since his prophecy was less clear, it had to be wrapped in many parables and images (on the cessation of prophecy, see below 18).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":2,"en":"The Sages said (Ḥagiga 13b, and Rashi) that Yeshayahu, who lived in Jerusalem and was active many years before the destruction, is considered a \"city dweller\" who is used to seeing the king and sees no need to describe his chariot. Whereas Yeḥezkel, who lived in Babylonia in the period of the destruction of the Temple, is considered a \"villager\" who is greatly impressed at seeing the king and describes his chariot in great detail. Prophecy also depends on the level of the prophet, as the Radbaz explained (2:816): the more he prepared himself, the clearer his prophecy will be, with fewer parables and riddles."}]},{"slug":"20-16-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-05/","title_he":"ה – הנבואה לישראל","title_en":"Prophecy for Israel","body_en":"<p>The Sages said (Kiddushin 70b) that God causes His Shekhina to rest \"upon families of distinguished lineage in Israel,\" and not upon families that contain those unfit to marry into the community, who are not fit for prophecy. Righteous converts too are included in Israel, who are fit for prophecy (Tosafot Ha-Rosh and Maharit there). And we have likewise learned that Ovadya the prophet was an Edomite convert (Sanhedrin 39b). And some say that the main purpose of the prophecy of righteous converts is for the sake of the nations of the world (see Kuzari 1:115; Meshekh Ḥokhma, Devarim 18:15; Peninei Halakha, Giyur 1:6).</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Tanna De-vei Eliyahu Rabba 9): \"I call heaven and earth to witness: whether Israelite or gentile, whether man or woman, whether manservant or maidservant – all according to the deeds that one does, so does the holy spirit rest upon him.\" However, so that no division should arise in the revelation of the faith in God's unity and of the Torah, prophecy that deals with the fundamentals of the Torah was given to the people of Israel alone, and this is what is stated (Shemot 33:16): \"So that we are distinguished, I and Your people, from all the people that are on the face of the earth\" (Berakhot 7a). Our teacher and rabbi, Rav Tzvi Yehuda Ha-Kohen Kook, of blessed memory, taught that as a rule, prophecy is a level that belongs to Israel, whereas <i>ru'aḥ ha-kodesh</i> (the holy spirit) is a level that belongs to Israel and to all the nations.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Sifrei on Devarim 18:15: \"'A prophet from your midst' – and not outside the Land. 'Of your brethren' – and not of others. 'Will the Lord your God raise up for you' – and not for the gentiles.\" And so it is explained in Berakhot 7a that God agreed to the request of Moshe Rabbeinu that the Shekhina should not rest upon the nations, and so it emerges from Bava Batra 15b that after Moshe Rabbeinu it is not possible for the Shekhina to rest upon the nations of the world. However, we have learned in Tanna De-vei Eliyahu Rabba 9 that the holy spirit rests upon gentiles too according to their deeds. And so wrote the Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 9:1. But prophecy that deals with the Torah, from the Revelation at Mount Sinai onward, was given to Israel alone, so that there would be one centre that preserves and reveals the faith in God's unity (Li-nvukhei Ha-dor 8; 14:1). And the Rambam wrote in the Epistle to Yemen that what is explained in the Sifrei – \"a prophet from your midst\" and not from the nations – means that God agreed that prophecy of foretelling the future would be only in Israel, but prophecy intended to arouse people to believe in the Torah of Moshe can come from any of the nations. My teacher and rabbi, the Ratziya (Rav Tzvi Yehuda Kook), explained many times that prophecy is unique to Israel, whereas the holy spirit was given to the nations as well (Mi-tokh Ha-Torah Ha-go'elet, vol. 3, p. 87; Siḥot Ha-Ratziya, Shemot, p. 305). For more on the holy spirit among the nations of the world, see above 9:1, n. 1.<br>It is stated (Devarim 34:10): \"And there has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moshe,\" and the Sages said (Sifrei): \"In Israel none arose, but among the nations of the world one arose. And who is this? This is Bil'am son of Be'or.\" They further said (Tanḥuma, Balak 1) that God gave prophecy to Bil'am so that the nations of the world would have no pretext to claim that had they merited a prophet they would have wanted the Torah as Israel did. The Shelah explained (Balak, Torah Or 6) that God made the one corresponding to the other: just as Moshe Rabbeinu rose in his apprehensions in the worlds of holiness, so, correspondingly, Bil'am attained his apprehensions by way of the worlds of impurity. And so wrote the Seder Ha-yom (end of the Order of Counting the Omer): that Moshe merited almost all the gates of purity (forty-nine gates), and correspondingly Bil'am was defiled in all fifty gates of impurity; and so wrote Rav Netanel Ḥayim Papa (Dover Meisharim, Parashat Vayikra), and others. On the other hand, some explain that Bil'am did not reach the level of Moshe Rabbeinu, but only by chance received prophecies that entered the Torah for the honour of Israel (Ibn Ezra, Ramban and Rabbeinu Baḥya)."}]},{"slug":"20-16-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-06/","title_he":"ו – הנבואה בארץ ישראל","title_en":"Prophecy in the Land of Israel","body_en":"<p>Since prophecy is a revelation of the Shekhina for the purpose of repairing the world, the principal place of prophecy is the Land of Israel, the Holy Land, which is designated for the revelation of God's word to the world; and in the future, too, all the peoples will unite around it in their faiths and religions, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 2:2-4): \"And it shall come to pass in the end of days that the mountain of the Lord's house shall be established at the top of the mountains and shall be exalted above the hills, and all the nations shall flow to it. And many peoples shall go and say: Come, let us go up to the mountain of the Lord, to the house of the God of Ya'akov, and He will teach us of His ways and we will walk in His paths; for out of Zion shall go forth Torah, and the word of the Lord from Jerusalem. And He shall judge between the nations and shall decide for many peoples; and they shall beat their swords into ploughshares and their spears into pruning hooks; nation shall not lift up sword against nation, nor shall they learn war any more\" (above 9:7).</p>\n<p>So indeed, in practice, almost all the prophets lived in the Land of Israel. And Jerusalem, the city of holiness and of the Temple, was the centre of prophecy, as the Sages said: any prophet whose city is not specified, \"it is known that he is from Jerusalem\" (Megilla 15a). By contrast, outside the Land there is almost no possibility of prophesying, as the Sages said: \"The Shekhina is not revealed outside the Land.\" Therefore, when Yona the prophet wished to stop prophesying about Nineveh, he left the Land, as it is stated (Yona 1:3, 10): \"But Yona rose up to flee to Tarshish from before the Lord… for he was fleeing from before the Lord\" (Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael, Bo; and see Mo'ed Katan 25a).</p>\n<p>It is true that there were prophets who prophesied outside the Land, but they merited this by virtue of being connected to the Land of Israel. As Rabbi Yehuda Halevi explained: \"Whoever prophesied, prophesied only in this Land or for its sake.\" Thus the first prophecy of our forefather Avraham, which was outside the Land, was a prophecy to go to the Land of Israel. And likewise Yeḥezkel, who lived in Babylonia, prophesied about the Land of Israel. In addition, Yeḥezkel had still seen the First Temple and the glory of the Shekhina, \"which, all the days that it rested in that House, every person who was prepared for it by virtue of his special quality would attain prophecy,\" so that his prophecy was drawn from the Land of Israel and from the Temple. Moshe, Aharon and Miriam, too, who prophesied in Egypt, prophesied about the Exodus from Egypt, which was intended to bring Israel to the Land of Israel. In addition, their prophecy was before the entry into the Land, and the Sages said (there): \"Until the Land of Israel was chosen, all lands were fit for the divine word (prophecy); once the Land of Israel was chosen, all the other lands were excluded.\" In addition, the places in Egypt where they prophesied were close to the border of the Land of Israel (Kuzari 2:14; and see there 4:10, 17. On the state of prophecy at the time of the destruction, see below, halakhot 8 and 18).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-07/","title_he":"ז – תנאי הנביא","title_en":"The Prerequisites of a Prophet","body_en":"<p>Among the prerequisites of prophecy is that the prophet possess wisdom and good character traits, for every deficiency a person has in wisdom or in the refinement of his character creates a partition between him and God, as it is stated (Yeshayahu 59:2): \"Your iniquities have separated between you and your God, and your sins have hidden His face from you, so that He does not hear,\" and when the partitions multiply he cannot receive prophecy. However, there is no righteous person on earth who has no flaws in understanding or in character, and every flaw a prophet has creates a partition that clouds the clarity of his prophecy. This is the basis of the difference between the levels of the prophets: the more perfect the prophet is in his wisdom, and the more his character traits are free of flaws, the clearer the prophetic apprehension he merits (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:1, 7; Introduction to Avot, chapter 7; Guide for the Perplexed 2:45).</p>\n<p>The Sages said: \"The Holy One, blessed be He, causes His Shekhina to rest only upon one who is mighty, wealthy, wise, humble and of stature\" (Nedarim 38a; Shabbat 92a). Beyond the simple interpretation, which will be brought below, the Rambam explained that this refers to the refinement of character, so that the prophet can receive the prophecy without any bias. As the Sages said (Avot 4:1): \"Who is mighty? One who conquers his inclination… Who is wealthy? One who is happy with his portion.\" Accordingly, the mighty one is he who rules over his character traits, so that anger or desire do not bias his judgement and conduct; rather, he will be angry or insist on his honour only for an educational or public need, and will enjoy the pleasures of the world in the measure fitting to gladden him and enrich his emotions, and no more. The wealthy one is he who is happy with what he has and does not need gifts that might bias his judgement. The trait of humility is needed so that he can think with integrity, and not bend his opinion so that his words will find favour in the eyes of others. And the Sages likewise said (Pesaḥim 66b): \"Whoever becomes arrogant or angry – if he is a sage, his wisdom departs from him; if he is a prophet, his prophecy departs from him.\"</p>\n<p>In practice too the prophet needs to be mighty and wealthy, so that he will be respected in the eyes of the public and they will accept the word of God from his mouth; for if he is poor or weak, he will need their help and they will not respect him (Derashot Ha-Ran, sermon 5; Ha-ikarim 3:10-12). From the aspect of character as well, as long as a person is poor or weak, his humility is not evident, for perhaps if he were wealthy and strong he would sin through pride and desire; but when he is wealthy and mighty and nevertheless possesses good character traits, it becomes clear that his traits are indeed truly pure (Ḥatam Sofer, and Ben Yehoyada on Nedarim 38a). In addition, since prophecy is a divine revelation, in order to receive it the prophet must express in full the image of God within him, and for this he must be whole in body and soul, mighty and wealthy (Akedat Yitzḥak, Shemot 35; Ein Aya, Shabbat there).</p>\n<p>It is true that there were times when God revealed Himself on a temporary basis to people who were not fit for prophecy, when He wished to warn them or arouse them about a particular matter (Rashba 1:548). Thus God warned Avimelekh king of Gerar to return our matriarch Sara to Avraham (Bereshit 20:3-7), and revealed Himself to Lavan the Aramean and warned him not to harm our forefather Ya'akov (there 31:24). And likewise an angel of the Lord revealed himself to Manoaḥ, even though he was an ignoramus, to instruct him about the nezirut of his son Shimshon (Shoftim 13:13; Berakhot 61a). Even to Bil'am's she-donkey an angel of the Lord revealed himself for the need of the hour, and God even opened the mouth of the donkey so that she spoke (Bamidbar 22:23-28).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"It seems that the cases in which God spoke to people who were not fit for prophecy were in a period when prophecy rested upon the world, when at times there was a kind of prophetic illumination, for the need of the hour, to people who were not prophets (Ha-ikarim 3:11). And according to the Rambam, Guide for the Perplexed 2:41, this was not prophecy but a certain level of the holy spirit; and so wrote Sforno and Malbim on Bereshit 20:3. And the Rambam follows his own view (Guide 2:32) that it is not possible for a person who is not wise and moral to prophesy. The Rashba 4:234 disagreed with him, for God has no limitations and He can grant prophecy even to an ordinary person. And so wrote the Ran (Derashot 4), Abarbanel (Amos 1:1) and others. Indeed, many explained that the qualities of might and wealth were required of permanent prophets, whose words had to be heeded by the public, but for temporary prophecy they were not needed (Re'em, Rosh on Nedarim 38a; Rashba 1:548). Thus, on a temporary basis God may choose to grant prophecy to any person according to the need of the hour, but it is not possible that a person who is unfit for prophecy in terms of his wisdom and righteousness should receive prophecies on a permanent basis like one of the prophets. And some say that one who exerts himself greatly to prepare himself in Torah and piety, in prayer and in <i>yiḥudim</i> (mystical unifications), can attain prophecy even without being mighty, wealthy and of stature (Ben Yehoyada, Nedarim there). One may say that since everyone knows that he is exceedingly righteous, they will accept his words.<br>In the period of prophecy, those who wished to rise and merit prophecy would attach themselves to the prophets and learn from them the ways of God and Torah, and they were called \"the sons of the prophets\" (see I Shmuel 10:5; II Melakhim 2:3-5; 4:1; 6:1). The Rambam wrote in the Guide 2:32 that, unlike the view of the philosophers who hold that any person who refines himself will necessarily attain prophetic spiritual apprehension, even a person who has made all the preparations will receive prophecy only if God wills it. End quote. However, regarding the holy spirit, he wrote in Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:1 that whoever merits becoming wise, whose mind is broad and whose character traits are rectified, immediately merits the holy spirit."}]},{"slug":"20-16-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-08/","title_he":"ח – מצב קבלת הנבואה","title_en":"The State Required for Receiving Prophecy","body_en":"<p>Even one who is perfect in his wisdom, his character traits and his bearing, to the point that he has reached the level fit for prophecy, cannot receive prophecy unless he is in a consciousness of faith and in a frame of mind of tranquillity together with vitality and joy. As the Sages said (Shabbat 30b): \"The Shekhina rests neither amid sadness, nor amid laziness, nor amid laughter, nor amid levity, nor amid idle conversation, nor amid frivolous matters, but only amid the joy of a mitzva.\" For when a person is in sadness or lacks vitality, faith is not properly revealed in him, and consequently he cannot merit prophecy.</p>\n<p>And thus it is told of Elisha the prophet that when the righteous Yehoshafat king of Yehuda came to him together with Yehoram son of Aḥav king of Israel, he was angry at Yehoram, who worshipped idols, to the point that he could not prophesy. But since he wished to prophesy for the sake of Yehoshafat, he asked that music be played before him in order to dispel his anger. As it is stated (II Melakhim 3:15): \"But now bring me a minstrel. And it came to pass, when the minstrel played, that the hand of the Lord came upon him.\" And we have likewise learned regarding our forefather Ya'akov that after the sale of Yosef, because of his great mourning and grief, he did not merit the holy spirit. Only after he heard that Yosef was alive did the holy spirit once again rest upon him, as it is stated: \"And the spirit of Ya'akov their father revived\" (Bereshit 45:27, and Targum Yonatan; Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:4).</p>\n<p>Accordingly, when Israel is in exile and subjugation, there is great sorrow in the world, and it is not possible to receive prophecy. In addition, the state of subjugation means that Israel cannot express its full talent, and consequently cannot prophesy, as it is stated (Eikha 2:9): \"Her king and her princes are among the nations, there is no Torah; her prophets also find no vision from the Lord.\" And the Rambam likewise wrote that there is no greater sadness and laziness for a person than his being a bondservant subjugated to ignorant people possessed of bestial desires (Guide for the Perplexed 2:36).</p>\n<p>When a person merits prophecy, his fellow prophets are aware of it, and those around him receive inspiration from his prophecy (see Sanhedrin 89b). And when a great prophet arises, the wellspring of prophecy grows stronger, and his disciples merit to prophesy. The more those who prophesy multiply, the more the circle of people who can rise and merit prophecy grows and widens. So it was in the days of Shmuel the prophet: by virtue of his work in bringing the people back in repentance and in teaching Torah and the ways of prophecy, prophecy began to be widespread in Israel (see I Shmuel 3:1; 19:20-24; Kuzari 1:103).<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"The Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:2): \"And all of them, when they prophesy, their limbs tremble, the strength of the body fails, their thoughts are confounded, and the mind is left free to understand what it sees\" (except for Moshe Rabbeinu, who remained standing as he was). Some explain that the prophet rises above his emotions and his worldly affairs until he is alone with the intellect alone (Rabbeinu Baḥya, Vayikra 8:8; Shelah, Masekhet Shavuot, Torah Or. And Sefer Ha-ikarim 3:10 detailed four levels of this). On the other hand, many did not agree with the Rambam, for we find that many prophets prophesied while speaking with people, such as Shmuel when he was asked about the donkeys, or when he anointed David. And as for what we find, that the prophet was sometimes in dread, this was because of the content of his prophecy. However, they too agree that great prophecies were revealed in a kind of dream in which a person is entirely concentrated in the imagination (Or Hashem of Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas 3:7:2; Rabbi Efrayim Ankawa, Sha'ar Kevod Hashem, introduction to the visions of prophecy 6; Rabbi Shem Tov ibn Shem Tov on the Guide 2:41; Abarbanel, Ma'ayanei Ha-yeshu'a, Ma'ayan 3:3; and others). See below 17:1, n. 1; according to what is explained there, it is possible that all would agree that when receiving prophecy the prophet's emotions are nullified, whereas when receiving the holy spirit they are not nullified. However, they disagreed with what the Rambam wrote regarding the state of the body."}]},{"slug":"20-16-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-09/","title_he":"ט – מדרגת משה ושאר הנביאים","title_en":"The Level of Moshe and of the Other Prophets","body_en":"<p>Moshe Rabbeinu was singular and unique. God planted his special soul in the generation of the birth of the Israelite nation, and he too refined his character traits and gave his life for Israel and for the honour of God, until he merited being God's emissary to bring Israel out of Egypt and to receive the Torah at Mount Sinai. The level of his prophecy was above that of all the other prophets, as it is stated (Devarim 34:10): \"And there has not arisen a prophet since in Israel like Moshe, whom the Lord knew face to face.\" And we have likewise learned from the sin of Miriam and Aharon, who did not understand the unique level of Moshe their brother, and said: \"Has the Lord indeed spoken only with Moshe? Has He not spoken with us also?\" In response God rebuked them and explained to them that the level of Moshe's prophecy is distinct from that of the other prophets: \"Hear now My words: If there be a prophet among you, I the Lord make Myself known to him in a vision, I speak with him in a dream. Not so My servant Moshe; he is trusted in all My house. Mouth to mouth I speak with him, plainly and not in riddles, and the likeness of the Lord he beholds. Why then were you not afraid to speak against My servant Moshe!?\" (Bamidbar 12:2, 6-8).</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Yevamot 49b): \"All the prophets gazed through a non-luminous speculum; Moshe Rabbeinu gazed through the luminous speculum.\" That is to say, prophetic seeing is like seeing through glass or a mirror. When it is clear, it is called \"a luminous speculum,\" and when it is cloudy, it is called \"a non-luminous speculum.\" Therefore the other prophets received their prophecy in parables and riddles that required deciphering and definition, whereas Moshe merited receiving a completely clear prophecy, to the point that all its details were revealed to him with the utmost precision from the mouth of the Almighty (see Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:2-6; Da'at Zekenim of the Ba'alei Ha-Tosafot, Bamidbar there; Derekh Hashem, part 3, chapter 5:1-5).</p>\n<p>A further difference between Moshe Rabbeinu and the other prophets is that none of the prophets could prophesy whenever they wished; only if they purified and sanctified themselves and God willed it did they merit prophecy. But Moshe Rabbeinu merited prophecy whenever it was needed. In accordance with the great stature of his mission, Moshe Rabbeinu had to be always ready and prepared to receive prophecy. Therefore, at the conclusion of the Revelation at Mount Sinai, God said to him: \"Go say to them (to Israel): Return to your tents (to marital joy); but as for you, stand here with Me,\" that is to say, from now on remain with Me and separate from your private affairs and from your wife, so as to be always ready to receive the words of the Torah (Devarim 5:27-28; Avoda Zara 5a). Therefore the other prophets did not separate from their wives, whereas Moshe Rabbeinu separated from his wife (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:6).</p>\n<p>Moshe's prophecy was unique in that through the Revelation at Mount Sinai all Israel witnessed the exalted level of his prophecy, as it is stated (Shemot 19:19): \"And when the voice of the shofar grew louder and louder, Moshe spoke, and God answered him by a voice.\" By virtue of this they accepted with absolute faith the Torah that God gave them through Moshe, as will be explained in the next halakha. Moreover, the prophecy of the prophets after him also continues his prophecy, as it is stated (Devarim 18:18): \"I will raise up for them a prophet from among their brethren, like you\" (below, halakhot 11-12).<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"Rabbeinu Ḥananel wrote (Yevamot 49a): \"All the prophets gazed through the non-luminous one, and it seemed to them that they saw a vision; this is like an old man whose sight is weak, who sees what is low as though it were high, and one as two, and the like, but it is not so. And this is what is written (Hoshea 12:11): 'and by the hand of the prophets I use similitudes' – the vision they see is an image and not the essence. And Moshe gazed upon the glory and splendour of the Shekhina through the luminous speculum, from behind the Shekhina.\" And some explain that the prophecy of the other prophets was through the power of the imagination, and Moshe's prophecy through the intellect (Abarbanel in Ma'ayanei Ha-yeshu'a, Ma'ayan 3:3; Malbim, Shemot 3:2).<br>The Rambam wrote that all the prophets saw their prophecy through an angel, whereas Moshe Rabbeinu saw directly (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:6; Guide for the Perplexed 2:34-35). And according to the Ramban (Bereshit 18:1), the prophecy of the other prophets too can be directly from God. Rabbi Efrayim Ankawa explained (end of Sha'ar Kevod Hashem) that what the Rambam means by \"angel\" is the medium of intellect and imagination that conveys the words of God to the prophet through a vision of parable and riddle, and he wrote that the Ramban too would agree to this.<br>The Sages said (Sifrei, Beha'alotkha 68) that whenever Moshe Rabbeinu wished, God would speak with him. And so wrote the Rambam (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:6). On the other hand, the Sages said (Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael, Parashat Bo) that during all those thirty-eight years in which God was angry with Israel because of the sin of the spies, God did not speak to Moshe; only after all the sinners had died did He speak again. This is one of the reasons that Tu Be-Av is considered a festive day for Israel, for then the sinners who had not wished to enter the Land ceased dying (Bava Batra 121a). Some explain that during those years God spoke with Moshe only for the needs of the hour (Rashbam and Ritva, Bava Batra there, in their second explanation). And some say that \"He did not speak with him in the way of affection as at first\" (Ritva in his first explanation; Tashbetz, Magen Avot). And some say that during those years God did not turn to Moshe in speech, but if Moshe turned to Him, God answered him (Maharshashakh, Kos Ha-yeshu'ot, Bava Batra there). And some say that during those years he would receive prophecy whenever he wished, but at the level of the other prophets (Rabbeinu Baḥya, Devarim 2:16-17)."}]},{"slug":"20-16-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-10/","title_he":"י – מעמד הר סיני יסוד התורה","title_en":"The Revelation at Mount Sinai, Foundation of the Torah","body_en":"<p>At the Revelation at Mount Sinai all Israel merited the most sublime divine revelation on earth, in order to verify for them with complete certainty the faith in God, and the faith in the Torah – that it is from God and that Moshe Rabbeinu is God's emissary to receive it on behalf of Israel. As it is stated (Shemot 19:9): \"And the Lord said to Moshe: Behold, I come to you in the thick cloud, so that the people may hear when I speak with you, and may also believe in you forever.\" And it is stated (Devarim 5:4-5): \"Face to face the Lord spoke with you on the mountain out of the midst of the fire. I stood between the Lord and you at that time, to declare to you the word of the Lord.\"</p>\n<p>Because of the greatness of the divine revelation at Mount Sinai, all the senses of Israel merged, to the point that they saw the sounds and heard God calling to Moshe Rabbeinu, and heard all at once all the Ten Commandments in one divine utterance. Afterwards they heard in detail the first two commandments of the Ten Commandments, in which the foundations of the faith of Israel are set out (Makkot 24a). However, their souls almost departed from them, as it is stated (there [Shemot] 20:15-16): \"And all the people saw the sounds and the flames, and the sound of the shofar, and the mountain smoking; and when the people saw it, they trembled and stood afar off. And they said to Moshe: You speak with us, and we will hear; but let not God speak with us, lest we die.\" Therefore they heard the following eight commandments with the help of Moshe, as it is stated: \"Moshe spoke, and God answered him by a voice\" – \"the Holy One, blessed be He, assisted him by giving him strength, so that his voice would be powerful and audible\" (Rashi, 19:19; 20:1, based on the Mekhilta there. See further above 1:7-8).<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"The Revelation at Mount Sinai established with certainty the faith in God, in His Torah and in Moshe His prophet (Kuzari 1:25, 87; Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 8:1; Semag, introduction to the positive commandments; above 1:8). However, since not all of Israel were at the level fit for prophecy, the question arose of how Israel received the prophetic revelation: did they hear all Ten Commandments explicitly from the mouth of God, or only some of them, and in a graduated prophetic revelation? In the Torah it is stated several times that God spoke all Ten Commandments to Israel (Devarim 4:13; 5:19; 10:4; and elsewhere). On the other hand, it is explained in Makkot 24a and in Horayot 8a that Israel heard only the first two commandments from the mouth of the Almighty, for only in these two did God address Israel in the first person, while the other commandments were stated in the third person.<br>According to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi in Shir Ha-shirim Rabba 1, Israel heard the first two commandments directly from God, and the rest they heard from Moshe. And so wrote Rashi, Shemot 19:19; Ḥizkuni, Shemot 20:1; Re'em, Bamidbar 15:22; Sefer Ha-likutim of the Arizal, Tehillim 62. And according to many, God spoke the first two commandments to Israel directly, and the other commandments He spoke to Moshe in such a way that Israel heard; and so wrote Sefer Ha-ikarim 3:18; Radbaz 2:817; Ma'asei Hashem 2, Yitro 6; Be'er Sheva, Horayot 8a; Rav Samson Raphael Hirsch, Bamidbar 15:22. Similar to this is the Ramban, Shemot 20:7.<br>According to these views, it is agreed that God first spoke all Ten Commandments to Israel all at once, and afterwards spelled out the first two for them, as explained in the Mekhilta de-Rabbi Yishmael (Yitro, Masekhta De-va-ḥodesh, parasha 9): \"'All these words' – this teaches that He spoke the Ten Commandments in one utterance, something that flesh and blood cannot say, as it is stated: 'And God spoke all these words, saying.' If so, what does the verse come to teach with 'I am the Lord your God – You shall have no'? Rather, it teaches that the Holy One, blessed be He, said the Ten Commandments in one utterance, and then went back and spelled them out, each utterance by itself.\" The Shelah explained (Masekhet Shavuot, chapter Torah Or 195) that Israel heard all Ten Commandments from the mouth of God, since they contain in essence all 613 mitzvot, and God wished that Israel hear with their own ears the essence of all 613 mitzvot, and thereby believe in the prophecy of Moshe, who would spell out for them the 613 mitzvot in full.<br>By contrast, according to the Sages in Shir Ha-shirim Rabba 1, all Ten Commandments were spoken to Israel in detail by God; and so wrote the Rashbam, Shemot 20:16; Ibn Ezra there 20:1; Ralbag there 20:15; Akedat Yitzḥak, Devarim 89; Abarbanel, Shemot 20; Maharal, Tiferet Yisrael 43; and others. And the eight commandments were stated in the third person in keeping with the style of the Torah, as we find in other places in the Torah that God speaks of Himself in the third person (Ibn Ezra). His wish was to tell Israel the foundations of the Torah and the acceptance of His kingship in direct language, and the rest in indirect language (Akedat Yitzḥak, Maharal). The Rambam (Guide for the Perplexed 2:33) has a unique view: that in the first two commandments Israel heard one continuous voice of many words without division between them, and Moshe explained their meaning to them, and the other commandments only Moshe told to Israel."}]},{"slug":"20-16-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-11/","title_he":"יא – נביא שהורה לבטל דבר מהתורה","title_en":"A Prophet Who Instructs to Abolish Something from the Torah","body_en":"<p>The role of the prophet is to continue in the way of Moshe Rabbeinu: to teach Torah to Israel, to strengthen them in observing the mitzvot, and to guide them in the ways of fulfilling them according to the needs of the hour, the community and the individual. But under no circumstances is he to uproot anything from the words of the Torah, for we have been commanded to fulfil the words of the Torah forever. As it is stated (Devarim 29:28): \"But the things that are revealed belong to us and to our children forever, that we may do all the words of this Torah.\" And it is stated (Devarim 13:1): \"All this word which I command you, that you shall observe to do; you shall not add to it, nor diminish from it.\" And it is stated (Vayikra 27:34): \"These are the commandments which the Lord commanded Moshe for the Children of Israel on Mount Sinai,\" to teach us \"that a prophet is not permitted to introduce anything new from now on,\" and this is what is stated (Devarim 30:12): \"It is not in heaven\" (Temura 16a). Accordingly, if a prophet says that something in the Torah must be changed – to annul a mitzva or to add a mitzva – it is known that he is a false prophet, and his punishment is death by strangulation.</p>\n<p>It makes no difference whether the false prophet lied knowingly or imagined in his heart that he had received a prophecy from God that contradicts the Torah. Since he claimed that something must be uprooted from the Torah, or that idolatry must be worshipped as a temporary measure, he is liable to death. For even if he imagined in his heart that he had received a prophecy about this, he should have known that the faith in God's unity and the Torah, which we all received together at the Revelation at Mount Sinai, are binding for all generations, and every imagining about various idols or about uprooting something from the Torah is a lie stemming from evil inclinations or from crooked thoughts that distort his inner images in an evil direction.</p>\n<p>Even one who has already been accepted as a prophet in Israel, after giving signs and wonders to prove his words (as explained below, halakha 13), if he instructs contrary to the Torah, it is forbidden to listen to him. As it is stated (Devarim 13:2-6): \"If there arises in your midst a prophet or a dreamer of dreams, and he gives you a sign or a wonder, and the sign or the wonder comes to pass, of which he spoke to you, saying: Let us go after other gods, which you have not known, and let us serve them – you shall not listen to the words of that prophet or to that dreamer of dreams… And that prophet or that dreamer of dreams shall be put to death, because he has spoken perversion against the Lord your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt and redeemed you from the house of bondage, to draw you away from the way in which the Lord your God commanded you to walk; so shall you remove the evil from your midst.\"</p>\n<p>Let us not wonder how it is possible for a false prophet to succeed in performing a sign or a wonder, for God gave man the possibility of choosing between good and evil, and of performing a wonder by trickery or by magical power (see below 19:4-5). And this is the test facing the listeners: to believe in God and His Torah, and not to believe a prophet who prophesies contrary to the Torah. As it is stated (there, 4): \"For the Lord your God is testing you, to know whether you love the Lord your God with all your heart and with all your soul.\"</p>\n<p>And although God gave the false prophet the ability to perform signs and wonders, all these signs and wonders do not compare with the receiving of the Torah at the Revelation at Mount Sinai. For any sign or wonder that a prophet performs does not prove absolutely that he is indeed a prophet, since there is always some concern that he performed it by sorcery or trickery; but as long as his words do not contradict the words of the Torah, one should assume that he is indeed a prophet, and there is a mitzva to heed his voice. This is just as we rely on two witnesses, even though it sometimes happens that two witnesses lie and the court does not succeed in its investigation in uncovering this. By contrast, all Israel took part in the Revelation at Mount Sinai; and with their whole being – their <i>nefesh</i>, <i>ru'aḥ</i> and <i>neshama</i> – they saw and heard how God gives the Torah to Israel through Moshe. Therefore a prophet who denies the Torah is a false prophet and is liable to death (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 8:1-3).</p>\n<p>However, in special situations, as a <i>hora'at sha'a</i> (a temporary measure), there is a mitzva to heed a prophet even when he instructs to transgress a mitzva of the Torah. Thus there was a mitzva to heed Eliyahu the prophet, who instructed to offer a sacrifice to God on Mount Carmel in order to sanctify God's name, even though from the time the Temple was built it was forbidden to offer a sacrifice anywhere else (Sanhedrin 89b). But if a prophet comes and instructs that it is permanently permitted to offer a sacrifice to God on Mount Carmel, since he has instructed to transgress a mitzva of the Torah permanently, it is known that he is a false prophet, and his punishment is death by strangulation. And likewise a prophet who instructs that it is permitted to worship idols, even as a temporary measure, it is known that he is a false prophet, and his punishment is death by strangulation (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 9:1, 4-5).<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"As a temporary measure, one is obligated to heed a prophet who instructs to transgress the words of the Torah. Similarly, we have learned that one is obligated to heed a court when it sometimes instructs to transgress the words of the Torah in order to strengthen Torah observance or to sanctify God's name (Yevamot 99b [?]; Rambam, Hilkhot Sanhedrin 24:4-10). The Rambam wrote in the Introduction to the Mishna: \"If a prophet whose prophecy has already been verified for us… were to tell us on the Shabbat day that we should all rise, women and men, kindle a fire, prepare weapons of war with it and gird them, and kill (the fighting) men of such-and-such a place today, which is Shabbat… we, who are commanded in the Torah of Moshe, would be obligated to rise immediately, without delay, as he commanded us, and do all that he commanded with alacrity and diligence, without doubt and without hesitation. And we would believe that everything we did on that day, which is Shabbat – kindling the fire, performing the labours, the killing and the war – is a mitzva. And we would hope to receive a reward for it from God, because we fulfilled the command of the prophet, which it is a positive mitzva to fulfil, as God commanded us through Moshe: 'to him you shall listen'… And indeed, a man who considers himself God-fearing and righteous, and is old and advanced in years, who says: Behold, I am very old, the years of my life are such-and-such, I have never transgressed any of the mitzvot, so how shall I rise today, which is Shabbat, and transgress a prohibition punishable by stoning and go to war? I neither add nor detract, and there are others in my place, and many are the people available to fulfil this command – that man transgresses the words of God, and is liable to death at the hands of Heaven, for he transgressed what the prophet commanded him\" (Rav Shilat edition, p. 35).<br>The Rambam's intention is a situation in which the temporary measure to transgress the words of the Torah strengthens Torah observance overall, such as a city that was at war with Israel, where at times, according to what was accepted in those days, it was necessary to kill all its fighters in order to deter the enemy and strengthen the security of Israel; but he is not speaking of an instruction that is contrary to the mitzvot of the Torah and its aims. For it is a rule that the prophets do not command commands that contradict the mitzvot of the Torah or its moral foundations, such as offering human sacrifices or sinning through forbidden sexual relations (Ta'anit 4a; Sanhedrin 93a; Arukh Ha-shulḥan He-atid, Shegagot 205:6). Accordingly, it seems that if a prophet commands something that we know with certainty to be immoral, we cannot believe that his words are words of prophecy. And even if he has proved with signs and wonders that he is a true prophet, we will assume that he is deceiving, or that his character traits are not rectified and therefore he became confused in receiving the prophecy (Rav Sa'adya Gaon, Emunot Ve-de'ot 3:8; see Poked Ikarim of Rabbi Tzadok, section 5).<br>Similarly, the Radbaz (2:652) at first raised the reasoning that in all matters that appear to contradict the moral rules of the Torah between one person and another, one should not heed the prophet. However, his conclusion is that since the temporary measure is in order \"to uphold the Torah and its other mitzvot, like the deed of Eliyahu,\" one must heed a prophet even in an instruction to shed blood and the like. Many Aḥaronim quoted his words. And so wrote Maran Ha-Rav Kook (Mishpat Kohen 144), adding that an ordinary statement of a prophet falls under the category of \"a fence for the matter\" and a sanctification of God's name even when we do not know how they act as a fence for the matter. However, as we have learned from their words, the Radbaz and those who agree with him also agree that if the matter clearly contradicts the aim of the Torah and its moral foundations, one should not heed him, for the temporary measure too must be in order to strengthen Torah observance as a whole. And indeed, we do not find among the prophets an example of an incomprehensible instruction. And some say that the law of forbidden sexual relations is like the law of idolatry, that one should not heed a prophet even as a temporary measure (Rabbi Yonatan Eybeschutz in Luḥot Edut; the second Admor of Komarno in Otzar Ha-ḥayim, Devarim 18:15). And some say that this is the law for all three of the severe transgressions about which one must always be killed rather than transgress, and one should not heed a prophet in them even as a temporary measure (Imrei Emet of Rabbi Yeḥiel Mikhel Goldschlag, Ḥiddushei Halakhot 99; Beit Ha-melekh, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 9:5; Sha'arei Torat Bavel on Sanhedrin 89b, by Rav Ze'ev Rabinovitch, may God avenge his blood). And some say that in all the mitzvot between one person and another one should not heed the prophet (Kli Ḥemda, Devarim 13:4)."}]},{"slug":"20-16-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-12/","title_he":"יב – מצווה לשמוע בקול נביא","title_en":"The Mitzva to Heed a Prophet","body_en":"<p>There is a mitzva to heed a prophet, as Moshe Rabbeinu said to the people of Israel (Devarim 18:15-18): \"A prophet from your midst, of your brethren, like me, will the Lord your God raise up for you – to him you shall listen. According to all that you asked of the Lord your God in Ḥorev on the day of the assembly, saying: Let me not hear again the voice of the Lord my God, and let me not see this great fire any more, lest I die. And the Lord said to me: They have spoken well in what they have spoken. I will raise up for them a prophet from among their brethren, like you, and I will put My words in his mouth, and he shall speak to them all that I command him.\" That is to say, this mitzva continues the Revelation at Mount Sinai: at first the divine revelation was to all of Israel, and when the souls of the Children of Israel nearly departed from them, they asked that Moshe speak with them and not God, and God agreed that Moshe's mediation was indeed needed, so that the words of the Torah could be absorbed in their minds and hearts. And God established that so it would be in future generations as well: when the generation is worthy, prophets will arise for them and guide them in the ways of the Torah.</p>\n<p>One who refuses to heed the prophecy of a prophet is liable to death at the hands of Heaven, as it is stated (there 18:19): \"And it shall come to pass that whoever will not listen to My words which he shall speak in My name – I will require it of him\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:7; 9:2-3). And it is likewise told that when Aḥav was negligent in defeating the king of Aram after his whole army had been struck, the prophet commanded a certain man: \"By the word of the Lord, strike me, please,\" so that he could deliver the rebuke to Aḥav while bruised and wounded. But despite the prophet's warning, the man persisted and defied his word. \"Then he (the prophet) said to him: Because you have not listened to the voice of the Lord, behold, as soon as you depart from me, a lion shall strike you. And as soon as he departed from him, a lion found him and struck him\" (I Melakhim 20:35-36; Kli Yakar of Aniyado). And another man heeded the prophet's voice and struck him and wounded him as the word of the Lord, and thus, wounded, he delivered the words of rebuke to the king: \"Thus says the Lord: Because you have let go out of your hand the man whom I had devoted to destruction, your life shall be for his life, and your people for his people\" (I Melakhim 20:42; Sanhedrin 89b).<sup>9</sup></p>\n<p>Likewise, a prophet who transgresses his own prophecy is liable to death at the hands of Heaven. So it happened to Iddo the prophet, who was sent from Yehuda to rebuke Yorovam son of Nevat for making the calves and for setting up the altar in Beit El, and was warned in his prophecy not to eat bread or drink water within the territory of the Kingdom of Israel. But a false prophet enticed him to eat, and the true prophet was punished in that a lion killed him. However, out of respect for the true prophet, the lion guarded his carcass until they took him for burial (I Melakhim chapter 13; Sanhedrin 89b).</p>\n<p>Likewise, a prophet who suppresses his prophecy and does not say it as the word of the Lord is liable to death. And so we have learned from the episode of Yona son of Amitai, who was commanded to prophesy calamity to the people of Nineveh in order to bring them to repentance. But Yona foresaw that from them the Assyrian empire would arise, which would destroy the Kingdom of Israel, and he sought to suppress his prophecy so that they would not repent and would be punished with annihilation. To this end he fled on a ship from the Land of Israel, the place of prophecy. But since the punishment of one who suppresses his prophecy is death at the hands of Heaven, he almost died in the storm and in the belly of the fish, and only after he repented and agreed to prophesy according to the word of the Lord was he saved (Yona chapters 1-2; Abarbanel 1:3; Sanhedrin 89a).</p>\n<p>The Sages said that when it is known about a prophet that he is suppressing his prophecy, he is flogged until he says it. And they asked: How do we know that he is suppressing his prophecy? And they answered that his fellow prophets are aware that he has received a prophecy, as it is stated (Amos 3:7): \"For the Lord God will do nothing, unless He reveals His secret to His servants the prophets.\" And although they themselves are not permitted to say his prophecy, and presumably also do not know it precisely, they know that he has received a prophecy, and on the basis of their words he may be flogged so that he will say it (Sanhedrin 89b; Tosafot there).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"The mitzva to heed a prophet applies only to what he prophesies in the name of God, and not to what he says on his own (Rashi; Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 9:2; Ramban; Abarbanel; and many others). However, according to Tosafot (Sanhedrin 89b, s.v. \"Eliyahu\"), there is a mitzva to heed a prophet even if he instructs to transgress the words of the Torah not by virtue of his prophecy, for he is no less than a court, which has the power to annul words of the Torah as a temporary measure (see Ḥinukh 495; Minḥat Ḥinukh 516:1).<br>In the Mishna (Sanhedrin 89a): \"One who disregards the words of a prophet… his death is at the hands of Heaven.\" Rashi explained: \"One who disregards… who treats them as ownerless, paying no heed to what the prophet told him.\" And so wrote the Meiri: \"He scorns his words and does not wish to do what he commands him.\" And so wrote the Rid, Rabbi Yehonatan of Lunel and others. However, the Rambam wrote (Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 9:2): \"one who transgresses\" the words of the prophet, and so wrote the Semag and the Ḥinukh. And the Minḥat Ḥinukh 516:2 wrote that according to them, anyone who does not fulfil the command of the prophet is punished with death at the hands of Heaven. However, it seems that the Rambam too would agree that one is punished only when transgressing the words of the prophet out of contempt, as he wrote in the Commentary on the Mishna (Sanhedrin 11:5): \"That he behave with disregard and contempt toward the commands of the prophets, and not fulfil them precisely and meticulously.\" Therefore I wrote above \"one who refuses to heed the prophet.\""}]},{"slug":"20-16-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-13/","title_he":"יג – מי נחשב נביא","title_en":"Who Is Considered a Prophet","body_en":"<p>The recognition that a person has become a prophet depends on the sages of Israel, who must examine him and recognise him as a prophet. Only afterwards is there a mitzva to heed his voice, and if he prophesies to uproot a mitzva from the Torah, he is liable to death. In order to accept him as a prophet, the sages must first examine whether he is fit for prophecy, that is, whether he is a great sage in Torah and in the understanding of life, cleaving to God and His mitzvot, possessed of good character traits and righteous in his deeds, praying for the welfare of Israel, a man of truth who works to bring sinners back in repentance and does not flatter the wicked (above, halakhot 2 and 7).</p>\n<p>After it has become clear that he is fit for prophecy, he must bring signs or wonders to prove that God sent him to prophesy. For so God established: when He wishes to send a prophet to Israel, He will give into his hand signs or wonders by which the people will know that he prophesies on God's mission. Thus God gave Moshe Rabbeinu three signs: a) turning the staff into a snake and back into a staff; b) turning his hand leprous by putting it into his bosom, and healing it by putting it into his bosom again; c) turning water from the Nile into blood. All this so that the Children of Israel would believe that he was a prophet sent to them from God (Shemot 4:1-9, 28-31; Rav Sa'adya Gaon, Emunot Ve-de'ot 3:4; Rav Hai Gaon; Derashot Ha-Ran 11). It is likewise possible for the prophet to prove that he is a prophet by foretelling, many times over, what is going to happen. If there was a change in even one thing of what he said, it is known that he is not a prophet. And if everything he said was fulfilled without any change, we believe that he is a prophet, and there is a mitzva to heed the voice of his prophecy. In this way they accepted Shmuel as a prophet, as it is stated (I Shmuel 3:19-20): \"And Shmuel grew, and the Lord was with him, and let none of his words fall to the ground. And all Israel from Dan to Be'er Sheva knew that Shmuel was established as a prophet of the Lord\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:1-2).</p>\n<p>The prophet must give signs until no argument remains that would cast doubt on their truth. Generally three signs suffice for this, as God gave into the hand of Moshe. And although it is possible that on rare occasions people will appear who seem wise and righteous and will succeed in performing signs or wonders by trickery or sorcery – if, according to the best understanding of the sages of Israel, he is a great, wise and righteous person and has given true signs, there is a mitzva to believe him and heed the voice of his prophecy (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 7:7).</p>\n<p>Likewise, when a renowned prophet testifies about his disciple that he is a prophet, he is accepted as a prophet without bringing signs, and there is a mitzva to heed his voice. Thus Moshe Rabbeinu testified about Yehoshua bin Nun and ordained him as a prophet, as it is stated (Bamidbar 27:18-21): \"And the Lord said to Moshe: Take for yourself Yehoshua bin Nun, a man in whom there is spirit, and lay your hand upon him… so that all the congregation of the Children of Israel may listen… at his word they shall go out and at his word they shall come in, he and all the Children of Israel with him, and all the congregation\" (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:5).<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"Rav Hai Gaon wrote (Teshuvot Ha-ge'onim, Sha'arei Teshuva 14) that since only the Great Sanhedrin of seventy-one can judge a false prophet, it follows that a person is not accepted as a prophet without its consent. However, from the unqualified language of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim it is implied that the acceptance of a prophet does not depend on the Sanhedrin. And one must say that when there is no Sanhedrin, the acceptance of the prophet is by the sages of Israel of that time. It should be added that the people too must agree to their acceptance, as the Ran explained (sermon 5): that the prophet must be wise, wealthy and mighty, so \"that he be chosen and accepted by all the factions – by the lovers of wisdom, the lovers of wealth and the lovers of might\" (above, halakha 7).<br>It has not been explained how many signs he must give for people to believe that he is a prophet. The question is important, for on the one hand he must give enough signs for people to believe that he is indeed a true prophet. On the other hand, after he has given enough signs, one is obligated to heed his voice and it is forbidden to test him further, and one who tests him transgresses a prohibition, as it is stated (Devarim 6:16): \"You shall not test the Lord your God, as you tested Him at Massa\" (Devarim 6:16; Ramban there; Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:5). The Ḥinukh 424 wrote: \"that he say things that are going to happen in the world two or three times.\" The book Turei Even on the Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:2, wrote that three times are needed, as the measure of a <i>ḥazaka</i> (established presumption), and as the number of signs God gave Moshe, and after that it is forbidden to test him. Several Aḥaronim copied his words. However, the Rambam wrote in Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:2: \"And we test him many times.\" And in his Introduction to the Mishna: \"And even if his words were fulfilled in one promise or two, his prophecy will not thereby be verified absolutely; rather, his prophecy will remain in suspense for us until the truth of all that he says in the name of God continues time after time.\" From the sum of their words I wrote as cited above. And so it emerges from the words of the Leḥem Mishneh and the Yad Peshuta there. And so it is implied by the words of Rabbi Ḥasdai Crescas (Or Hashem, ma'amar 2, 4:2), that no concern may remain that the sign is not from God. It seems one should further add, based on what is explained in note 8, that the more difficult it is, morally or in terms of Torah, to accept the prophecy, the more unmistakable the prophet's signs must be, until those who hear the prophet believe wholeheartedly that this is the will of God (see Rav Sa'adya Gaon, Emunot Ve-de'ot 3:8)."}]},{"slug":"20-16-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-14/","title_he":"יד – נביא שנבואתו לא התגשמה","title_en":"A Prophet Whose Prophecy Was Not Fulfilled","body_en":"<p>If, after a person has been accepted as a prophet, he prophesies about something that will happen and it does not happen, this is a sign that he has sinned and fallen from his level, and from then on he is forbidden to prophesy. And if he continues to prophesy, he is liable to death by the court. As it is stated (Devarim 18:21-22): \"And if you say in your heart: How shall we know the word which the Lord has not spoken? When a prophet speaks in the name of the Lord, and the thing does not follow nor come to pass – that is the thing which the Lord has not spoken; the prophet has spoken it presumptuously, you shall not be afraid of him.\"</p>\n<p>However, if he prophesied calamity and his prophecy was not fulfilled, there is no flaw in his prophecy, because this is the aim of prophecy – to bring sinners back in repentance – and thereby perhaps God will relent of the evil and the calamity will not come. And so it is stated in Yirmeyahu (18:7-8): \"At one moment I may speak concerning a nation and concerning a kingdom, to pluck up and to break down and to destroy; but if that nation, concerning which I have spoken, turns from its evil, I will relent of the evil that I thought to do to it.\" And so it happened with Yona, who prophesied that Nineveh would be overturned, and they repented and the decree was annulled (Yona 4:2).</p>\n<p>But a good prophecy is not annulled, as the Sages said (Berakhot 7a): \"Every single word that went forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, for good, even conditionally, He did not retract.\" Therefore, if a prophet's good prophecy was not fulfilled, it is known that he is a false prophet. And so Yirmeyahu the prophet said to Ḥananya son of Azur, who at first was a true prophet; when he began to prophesy about the salvation of Israel, contrary to Yirmeyahu's prophecies of destruction, Yirmeyahu suspected that he had become a false prophet. \"And the prophet Yirmeyahu said: Amen, may the Lord do so\" – that is to say, would that your good prophecy be fulfilled; but know that if it is not fulfilled, it will become known that you are a false prophet (Yirmeyahu 28:6-9. See below, halakha 16).</p>\n<p>However, when the good prophecy was said to the prophet and not to the public, it is possible that sin will cause the good thing to be annulled. As we have learned (Berakhot 4a) from our forefather Ya'akov, to whom God promised (Bereshit 28:15): \"And behold, I am with you, and I will guard you wherever you go, and I will bring you back to this land; for I will not leave you until I have done that which I have spoken to you.\" Nevertheless, our forefather Ya'akov feared that Esav would kill him and his whole family, as it is stated (there 32:8): \"Then Ya'akov was greatly afraid and distressed.\" The Sages explained that he feared that perhaps he had sinned and the promises he had received would be annulled. But had the good prophecy been said publicly, it would not have been annulled (Rambam, Hilkhot Yesodei Ha-Torah 10:4; Introduction to the Mishna; Ramban, Sefer Ha-ge'ula 2:2; Derashot Ha-Ran 2).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-15/","title_he":"טו – עונש המתנבא לשקר","title_en":"The Punishment of One Who Prophesies Falsely","body_en":"<p>As we have learned (in halakha 11), the Torah established a deterrent punishment for a false prophet – death by strangulation by the court. As it is stated (Devarim 18:20): \"But the prophet who presumes to speak a word in My name which I have not commanded him to speak… that prophet shall die.\" That is to say, if he prophesied to uproot one of the mitzvot of the Torah, he is warned to retract, and if he does not retract, the court punishes him with death. Similarly, a prophet who prophesied about something that was not fulfilled as he prophesied – since it has become clear that he failed and lost his standing as a prophet, the sages of Israel warn him not to continue prophesying. And if he continues to prophesy in the name of God, he is a false prophet and liable to death.</p>\n<p>Since this concerns a person who is regarded in people's eyes as a prophet, the Torah commanded that we not be afraid to punish him, as it is stated (Devarim 18:22): \"You shall not be afraid of him,\" and that we not hesitate to find him guilty, for \"the prophet has spoken it presumptuously.\" That is to say, one must not argue on his behalf that his imagination misled him; rather, he must be regarded as a deliberate sinner (Sifrei; Abarbanel there). He is not judged by a Minor Sanhedrin of twenty-three judges, as others liable to death are judged, but by the Great Sanhedrin of seventy-one, whose seat is next to the Temple (Sanhedrin 16a; Rambam, Hilkhot Avoda Zara 5:9).</p>\n<p>One should note that there are two kinds of prohibitions with regard to prophecy. The first is not to violate the words of the prophet; one who transgresses this transgresses a mitzva between man and God, and therefore his punishment is death at the hands of Heaven (above, halakha 12). The second is not to prophesy falsely; one who transgresses this causes his fellows to sin and harms them, and therefore his punishment is at the hands of man, by death by strangulation in court. And if he also enticed to idolatry, that is, persuaded people to worship idols permanently, he is a <i>mesit</i> (inciter) and his death is by stoning (Radbaz 5:46; above 3:5).</p>\n<p>The Sefer Yere'im (siman 241) wrote that a person must take care not to say in jest: \"Such-and-such the Lord said to me.\" And although it is clear that his words are not considered false prophecy, for only a person who has become renowned as great in wisdom and deeds can be considered a prophet, nevertheless even an ordinary person is forbidden to disgrace prophecy by speaking like a prophet in jest (Minḥat Ḥinukh 517:4).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-16/","title_he":"טז – הקושי להבחין בין נביא אמת לנביא שקר","title_en":"The Difficulty of Distinguishing Between a True Prophet and a False Prophet","body_en":"<p>Having learned the general rules of the laws of prophecy, we must return to the question of distinguishing between true prophets and false prophets. For \"God has made the one corresponding to the other\" – the good inclination corresponding to the evil inclination. Corresponding to the possibility of prophesying truly there is a possibility of prophesying falsely. That is to say, in addition to the prophets of idolatry, the sorcerers and the diviners, who by various magical powers foretold the future and performed wonders, there are also false prophets, who prophesied falsely, ostensibly in the name of God.</p>\n<p>There are two main kinds of false prophets. The first lies knowingly, that is, invents prophecies according to what he estimates in his own mind, in order to increase his honour and rule over people, or in order to obtain benefits. Such a false prophet must be sophisticated and cunning, knowing how to use the information in his possession to show that he ostensibly knows hidden things and foretells the future, and thereby convince his listeners that he is a prophet; or he will have magical powers of seeing hidden things, with whose help he will convince people to believe in him. The second is a person who yearns to prophesy so much that he imagines his dreams and fantasies are prophecies, and becomes convinced that he is a true prophet and that the words of his prophecy will be fulfilled. Sometimes he will hear words of prophecy from true prophets, and inspired by them, fantasies will arise in his mind, and he will think they are true prophecies. And sometimes, out of inner conviction, he will even dare to argue with the true prophets that they are not righteous or wise enough, and therefore did not succeed in understanding their prophecy fully, whereas he, in his imagination, succeeded in receiving the true prophecy (Yirmeyahu 23:30-32, and Abarbanel there; see Yeḥezkel 14:9).</p>\n<p>The danger from false prophets is enormous, since they appear righteous, and seemingly there is a mitzva to heed their voice, but they lead the people to ruin, as happened in the generation preceding the destruction of the First Temple. The true prophets warned the kings and ministers not to rebel against Babylonia, but to apply themselves diligently to the moral repair of the people so that they would be worthy of salvation. And they prophesied that if they rebelled, they would bring about destruction, exile, sword and famine. By contrast, the false prophets encouraged the kings and the people to fight for their independence, promised that the Temple would not be destroyed, and even accused the true prophets, who called for repentance and prophesied the destruction, of lying. As Yirmeyahu the prophet said to God (14:13): \"Then I said: Ah, Lord God! Behold, the prophets say to them: You shall not see the sword, nor shall you have famine; but I will give you assured peace in this place.\" And he likewise warned the people (27:14-15): \"Do not listen to the words of the prophets who speak to you, saying: You shall not serve the king of Babylonia; for they prophesy a lie to you! For I have not sent them, says the Lord, yet they prophesy falsely in My name!\" And thereby they would cause the people to die \"by the sword, by famine and by pestilence, as the Lord has spoken concerning the nation that will not serve the king of Babylonia\" (there 27:13).</p>\n<p>Not only that, but there were true prophets who strayed from the path and became false prophets. Such was Ḥananya son of Azur, who rose up against Yirmeyahu and prophesied that within two years Yehuda would be freed from the yoke of Babylonia. The Sages said (Sanhedrin 89a) that at that time Ḥananya no longer received prophecy himself; rather, Yirmeyahu prophesied in the \"upper market\" of Jerusalem that Elam was about to be broken, and Ḥananya drew an a fortiori inference that Babylonia too would be punished, and went and prophesied in the \"lower market\" that the yoke of Babylonia was about to be broken in another two years. Yirmeyahu suspected him of lying, and warned him that if they were not redeemed in another two years, it would become clear that he was a false prophet. But Ḥananya persisted in claiming that he had received a prophecy about this, and Yirmeyahu was at a loss against him (there [Yirmeyahu] 23:1-11 [it seems this should be 28:1-11]).</p>\n<p>Some time later God revealed to Yirmeyahu in a prophecy that Ḥananya had become a false prophet, and sent Yirmeyahu to warn him. \"Then the prophet Yirmeyahu said to Ḥananya the prophet: Hear now, Ḥananya, the Lord has not sent you, and you have made this people trust in a lie. Therefore thus says the Lord: Behold, I will send you away from the face of the earth; this year you shall die, because you have spoken rebellion against the Lord. So Ḥananya the prophet died that same year in the seventh month\" (there 28:15-17). Seemingly Yirmeyahu's words were fulfilled, for Ḥananya died that year; but his death became known in the seventh month, Tishrei, after the next year had already begun. The Sages said (Yerushalmi Sanhedrin 11:5) that Ḥananya saw that he was about to die before Rosh Ha-shana, as Yirmeyahu had prophesied, and asked the members of his household to conceal his death until the next year had begun, so that people would continue to believe that his prophecy was true and Yirmeyahu's prophecy false. Here we have a man who at first cleaved to God and merited prophecy, and out of his mistaken belief that it was impossible for the Temple to be destroyed, strayed from his path and regarded his fantasies as prophecies. And he became so entrenched in this that even after he saw that the word of God through Yirmeyahu about his death was about to be fulfilled, he commanded that it be concealed, for he believed that if they strengthened themselves in their faith in the Temple and in the Kingdom of Yehuda, the king of Babylonia would not succeed in destroying Jerusalem.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-17/","title_he":"יז – התנאים להבחנה בין נביא אמת לשקר","title_en":"The Conditions for Distinguishing Between a True and a False Prophet","body_en":"<p>Although it is sometimes very difficult to distinguish between a true prophet and a false prophet, it seems that if the sages of Israel carry out the required examinations with integrity and rigour (above, halakha 13), they will succeed in distinguishing between them.</p>\n<p>First one must examine whether the personality of the one claiming prophecy is suited to it, that is, whether he is a Torah scholar of broad mind, possessed of good character traits and a speaker of truth (above, halakha 7). This examination, if carried out seriously, will remove almost all the impostors who deliberately claim to be prophets while knowing in their hearts that they are not, for it is not possible for them to hide their bad character from sages who know them personally. This examination will also remove most of the people who mistakenly believe themselves to be prophets. For one who mistakenly imagines that he has prophesied presumably is not careful about the trait of truth, and since he exaggerates and alters things in his speech, he should not be accepted as a prophet. In addition, one who has such fantasies presumably lacks the ability to criticise himself and his positions, and if his wisdom is properly examined, they will discern that he is not balanced in his understanding and his worldview.</p>\n<p>If despite this he has passed the preliminary test of personality, the stage will come at which he must prove his prophethood by performing wonders that alter nature, or by foretelling the future several times (above, halakha 13). It seems that if this examination is carried out fearlessly and rigorously, without waiving any detail, the sages of Israel will succeed in disqualifying the false prophets who passed the test of personality.</p>\n<p>However, when the generation is morally degraded, it stands to reason that the sages and leaders of the generation will find it hard to examine the prophet's personality with integrity, for the moral flaws in him will not jar them, and some of them will think that relative to others he is righteous and pious, even though his righteousness is unbalanced and he has flaws that do not allow him to be a prophet. In addition, when the public moral level is low, many of the people will be tempted to accept the words of \"prophecy\" of the false prophet, who knows how to cater to the public's taste, and therefore they will not be meticulous in examining whether the signs he gives are completely precise. So it happened in the period before the destruction of the First Temple: following the degraded moral state, many were tempted to believe the false prophets.</p>\n<p>And so it was when Aḥav king of Israel and Yehoshafat king of Yehuda planned to fight against Aram, and Yehoshafat asked to inquire of the prophets. Aḥav gathered four hundred prophets, and all of them advised him to go out to war and answered with one voice: \"Go up, and the Lord will deliver it into the hand of the king\" (I Melakhim 22:6). But Yehoshafat suspected that their prophecy was not true and asked for another prophet. His suspicion was aroused because they all prophesied in the same words, and it is a rule that prophecy is a personal revelation to the prophet, and therefore \"no two prophets prophesy in the same style\" (Sanhedrin 89a). That is to say, although many prophets can prophesy a prophecy of one content, each says it in his own language. They called Mikhayhu son of Yimla, who was a true prophet, and he prophesied that they would fail in the war and the king would die. Had they kept the rules of examining prophecy meticulously, they would have known that Mikhayhu was the true prophet, and the four hundred who spoke in one language were prophesying falsely. Moreover, it would have been proper to suspect that since these four hundred prophets were close to the royal court, they were not clean-handed and had not been examined for prophecy according to the rules of halakha. But because of the moral state of the kings and their advisers, they preferred to listen to the false prophets who prophesied good things about them, and to ignore the true prophet. Thus they failed in the war and King Aḥav died.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"20-16-18","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-18/","title_he":"יח – הסתלקות הנבואה","title_en":"The Departure of Prophecy","body_en":"<p>In the days of the Judges and the Kings, prophecy guided the people and elevated it. In the peak period, under the influence of Shmuel the prophet, the people repented, the monarchy was established and the First Temple was built. Afterwards too the prophets influenced the order of the kingdom for the good. But in a gradual process their influence diminished, and their rebukes did not succeed in bringing the people back in repentance. As it is stated (Yirmeyahu 7:25-26): \"Since the day that your fathers came out of the land of Egypt until this day, I have sent to you all My servants the prophets, daily rising early and sending them. Yet they did not listen to Me, nor incline their ear, but stiffened their neck; they did worse than their fathers.\" Many were drawn after the idols, and even when they came to the Temple and served God, they did not serve Him wholeheartedly by walking in His ways. Rather, just as idolaters entice their idols with sacrifices so that they will help them, so they brought sacrifices to God so that He would help them and they could continue in their evil way, oppressing the poor and growing rich (Yirmeyahu 7:9-11). But God wishes us to improve our deeds, to distance ourselves from evil and cleave to good, and thereby merit abundant blessing. As the prophet Yeshayahu said (1:11-20): \"To what purpose is the multitude of your sacrifices to Me? says the Lord… Wash yourselves, make yourselves clean; put away the evil of your doings from before My eyes; cease to do evil! Learn to do well (learn to do good to others); seek justice… judge the fatherless, plead for the widow… If you are willing and obedient, you shall eat the good of the land; but if you refuse and rebel, you shall be devoured by the sword.\" As the moral state deteriorated, the false prophets multiplied, who by the power of their imagination flattered the public and caused it to sin, and the influence of the true prophets diminished – until the sins prevailed, the prophecies of destruction were fulfilled, and Israel was exiled from its land.</p>\n<p>At the beginning of the Second Temple period the last of the prophets were still active, but they handed over the spiritual leadership to the Sages. This found expression in the figure of Ezra, whose name as a prophet was Malakhi (Megilla 15a), but who was known chiefly as Ezra the Scribe, because the essence of his work and leadership was as a sage who enacted ordinances that shaped the spiritual character of the people of Israel for all generations. By his power the batei midrash (houses of study) arose, which raised up many disciples, and in them grew the Sages who, by intensifying the study of the Oral Torah and establishing ordinances and protective fences, succeeded in eradicating from Israel the severe sins of idolatry, forbidden sexual relations and bloodshed, on account of which the First Temple was destroyed (Yoma 9b). Thus the legacy of the prophets passed to the Sages, as the Talmud states (Bava Batra 12a): \"From the day the Temple was destroyed, prophecy was taken from the prophets and given to the sages… and a sage is preferable to a prophet.\"</p>\n<p>Despite the terrible loss in the departure of prophecy, there was also a positive side to it, because as long as prophecy rested upon Israel, the evil inclination for idolatry and false prophecy was also enormous, and many were drawn after it. The Sages said that the power of this evil inclination was so great that the Men of the Great Assembly, headed by Ezra the Scribe, asked to abolish the inclination for idolatry from the world, and their request was granted (Yoma 69b; below 19:14). That is to say, in the transition from the period of the prophets to that of the Sages, a decision was made to forgo the aspiration to reach the lofty peaks of prophecy, which can also lead to false fantasies and to sins of idolatry, and instead to focus on precise Torah study according to wisdom. In this way the great light shining upon us from above was reduced, and in its place the small lights revealed in every topic of study shone brightly. Thus the Sages succeeded in rooting in Israel devotion to Torah and mitzvot, and in eradicating the severe sins. But together with the loss of the great prophetic illumination, the unified vision of the Torah was also impaired, and consequently the inclination toward division grew stronger, until, through the sin of baseless hatred, the Second Temple was destroyed and we were exiled from our land (Yoma 9b).<sup>11</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"See Orot by Maran Ha-Rav Kook, Zer'onim 2, the essay \"Ḥakham Adif Mi-navi\" (A Sage Is Preferable to a Prophet), and \"Le-mahalakh Ha-ide'ot Be-Yisrael,\" chapter 2. The festival of Ḥanukka is the festival of the Second Temple, and the Ḥanukka candles allude to the Oral Torah, as explained in Peninei Halakha, Zemanim 11:5-6. Rabbi Tzadok Ha-Kohen of Lublin elaborated on the abolition of the evil inclination for idolatry, which correspondingly brought about the cessation of prophecy (Resisei Laila 13:1; Poked Ikarim 6:21; and elsewhere), and so wrote the Gra on Seder Olam Rabba, chapter 30; Meshekh Ḥokhma, Bamidbar 11:17. And see Orot, Yisrael U-teḥiyato 18."}]},{"slug":"20-16-19","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/20-16-19/","title_he":"יט – מורשת הנביאים","title_en":"The Legacy of the Prophets","body_en":"<p>The prophets did not succeed in preventing the destruction and the exile, but they did succeed in establishing in the consciousness of Israel the understanding that the destruction and the exile were caused by the abandonment of the covenant with God and by moral sins, and that the way of repair is through repentance. By virtue of this, when Israel returned from the Babylonian exile and built the Second Temple, the Sages established the Oral Torah, and through its influence they succeeded in eradicating from Israel idolatry and the severe sins that stem from it, and in fortifying the observance of the mitzvot in Israel.</p>\n<p>In addition, the entire exalted legacy of the prophets has remained forever. Through all the peaks and falls, the prophets rooted in Israel the deep and pure faith in God, who does not demand that we offer thousands of rams in His honour and pour out before Him ten thousand rivers of oil, but that we walk in His ways: \"To do justice, and to love kindness, and to walk humbly with your God\" (Mikha 6:8). \"Thus says the Lord: Let not the wise man glory in his wisdom, nor let the mighty man glory in his might, let not the rich man glory in his riches. But let him who glories glory in this – that he understands and knows Me, that I am the Lord who exercises kindness, justice and righteousness in the earth, for in these things I delight, says the Lord\" (Yirmeyahu 9:22-23). Through this the prophets succeeded in planting in the consciousness of Israel the vision of the redemption of the world, in which we will all rise morally and the Shekhina will rest in Israel; we will settle the Land of Israel and add goodness and blessing to the world, and all the peoples of the world will stream to the mountain of the Lord's house, and from Israel they will receive inspiration to repair the whole world under the kingdom of Shaddai.</p>\n<p>Even in the difficult days of destruction and exile, the great vision of the prophets breathed hope into us and gave us strength to continue clarifying and refining the guidance of the Torah, until the dawn broke and the light of the Torah of the Land of Israel and the vision of the prophets began to appear in the ingathering of the exiles and the settling of the Land. And now we are called upon to join the general light of prophecy with wisdom and halakha in all their details and fine points, until the light of faith shines forth in its fullness, and wickedness and death vanish from the world, and God wipes away tears from all faces and removes from us the disgrace of exile, and all Israel will say: \"Behold, this is our God; we have waited for Him, and He will save us; this is the Lord, we have waited for Him, we will be glad and rejoice in His salvation\" (Yeshayahu 25:9).</p>","notes_en":[]}]});
